#
#   # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   # PUBLISHING IS OFF ON PURPOSE.
#   #
#   # Nothing has been released under the name `manul-browser` yet, and the first
#   # upload to PyPI is the one thing here that cannot be undone: a version number
#   # is burnt whether or not the file was any good, and yanking does not free it.
#   # So the pipeline stops after the smoke jobs and leaves the wheels as workflow
#   # artifacts, which is exactly what is wanted while the tags are still
#   # rehearsals.
#   #
#   # To turn it on, in this order:
#   #   1. Confirm on GitHub that the repository is named `manul-browser` — the
#   #      PyPI trusted publisher is matched on repository name, and a rename
#   #      after the fact silently breaks it.
#   #   2. Create the `pypi` environment on GitHub, and the pending publisher on
#   #      PyPI: project `manul-browser`, owner `alexbeatnik`, repo
#   #      `manul-browser`, workflow `release.yml`, environment `pypi`.
#   #   3. Uncomment `publish` below. Push a tag and check TestPyPI first if you
#   #      want a rehearsal that costs nothing — add `with: repository-url:
#   #      https://test.pypi.org/legacy/` to the publish step.
#   #   4. Uncomment `github-release` last. It carries `needs: [version, publish]`,
#   #      so it will not fire until publishing works; if you want the binaries on
#   #      a GitHub release *without* PyPI, change that to `[version, smoke]` and
#   #      uncomment this job alone.
#   #   5. Delete the "Not on PyPI yet" note at the top of
#   #      bindings/python/README.md — that file is the PyPI description, and it
#   #      would ship the disclaimer with the release that disproves it.
#   #
#   # Both jobs are known-unrun. They have never executed against a real tag.
#   # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
